1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:02,728
January fifth, nineteen fourteen.

2
00:00:03,529 --> 00:00:09,722
Before sunrise, ten thousand men are
standing in the freezing cold outside a
factory gate in Detroit.

3
00:00:10,521 --> 00:00:12,965
They have heard a rumor that sounds
impossible.

4
00:00:13,763 --> 00:00:19,267
Inside, one man has just made a decision
that newspapers around the world will call
madness.

5
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:23,889
He is about to double the wages of his
workers, in a single day,

6
00:00:24,102 --> 00:00:26,975
for no reason his own investors can
understand.

7
00:00:27,773 --> 00:00:29,462
His name is Henry Ford.

8
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:33,446
And the secret behind that decision did
not make him poorer.

9
00:00:34,245 --> 00:00:37,014
It made him the richest industrialist in
America.

10
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:43,404
Henry Ford was born on July thirtieth,
eighteen sixty-three, on a farm in
Michigan.

11
00:00:44,204 --> 00:00:46,908
His father expected him to inherit the
land.

12
00:00:47,707 --> 00:00:49,643
Henry wanted nothing to do with it.

13
00:00:50,441 --> 00:00:56,153
Farm work was slow, brutal, and endless,
and even as a boy he was obsessed with one
question:

14
00:00:56,418 --> 00:00:59,682
why does a human being have to do what a
machine could do instead?

15
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:05,593
At twelve years old, he saw a steam engine
moving down a country road on its own
power,

16
00:01:05,741 --> 00:01:07,286
with no horse in front of it.

17
00:01:08,084 --> 00:01:10,619
He later said that moment changed his
life.

18
00:01:11,418 --> 00:01:15,971
He jumped off the wagon and questioned the
driver until the man ran out of answers.

19
00:01:16,769 --> 00:01:18,849
A year later, his mother died.

20
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:23,575
Ford said the house was never the same,
like a watch without a spring.

21
00:01:24,374 --> 00:01:30,697
At sixteen he walked eight miles to
Detroit, with almost no money, and took a
job in a machine shop.

22
00:01:31,496 --> 00:01:35,033
He was not the best student, and he was
never the best talker.

23
00:01:35,832 --> 00:01:41,231
But he could take apart a watch and put it
back together, and he could do it better
than the men who sold them.

24
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:44,828
Ford spent his twenties working in the
shadows.

25
00:01:45,629 --> 00:01:51,757
In eighteen ninety-one he joined the
Edison Illuminating Company, and within
two years he was chief engineer.

26
00:01:52,556 --> 00:01:55,468
It was a good, safe, respectable life.

27
00:01:56,267 --> 00:02:01,510
But every night, in a small brick shed
behind his house, he was building
something else.

28
00:02:02,308 --> 00:02:09,648
On a rainy night in June eighteen ninety-
six, he finished it: a rattling, four-
wheeled machine he called the Quadricycle.

29
00:02:10,446 --> 00:02:14,622
It was so wide that he had to knock down
part of the shed wall to get it out.

30
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:16,979
Then came the failures.

31
00:02:17,777 --> 00:02:20,755
His first company collapsed in nineteen
hundred and one.

32
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:27,252
Investors lost their money, and some of
them started calling him a dreamer who
would never finish anything.

33
00:02:28,051 --> 00:02:30,182
His second attempt fell apart too.

34
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:37,330
The partners hired an outsider to fix the
company, and that outsider eventually put
his own name on the cars.

35
00:02:38,129 --> 00:02:39,583
It became Cadillac.

36
00:02:40,381 --> 00:02:44,687
Ford was thirty-eight years old, broke,
and publicly humiliated.

37
00:02:45,486 --> 00:02:47,994
So he did the one thing that fit his
character.

38
00:02:48,793 --> 00:02:49,856
He went racing.

39
00:02:50,655 --> 00:02:55,130
In October nineteen hundred and one, he
beat the most famous driver in America,

40
00:02:55,277 --> 00:02:57,812
Alexander Winton, in a single race.

41
00:02:58,611 --> 00:03:01,718
The crowd went wild, and money began to
listen.

42
00:03:02,517 --> 00:03:10,651
Two years later, in June nineteen oh-
three, Ford Motor Company was born, with
twelve investors and about twenty-eight
thousand dollars.

43
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:15,359
The early company sold cars to the rich,
like everyone else.

44
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:21,924
But Ford had a different idea, and he kept
repeating it: build a motor car for the
great multitude.

45
00:03:22,722 --> 00:03:25,049
Rich men's toys were not the future.

46
00:03:25,847 --> 00:03:30,700
A car so simple that a farmer could fix
it, and so cheap that a farmer could buy
it,

47
00:03:30,834 --> 00:03:31,832
was the future.

48
00:03:32,631 --> 00:03:35,908
In October nineteen oh-eight, the Model T
arrived.

49
00:03:36,706 --> 00:03:41,845
It cost eight hundred and fifty dollars,
it was tough, light, and easy to repair,

50
00:03:42,032 --> 00:03:44,619
and it could survive roads that barely
existed.

51
00:03:45,417 --> 00:03:47,353
America went crazy for it.

52
00:03:48,152 --> 00:03:51,038
Orders poured in faster than the factory
could build.

53
00:03:51,837 --> 00:03:54,111
Farmers stopped feeling isolated.

54
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:59,228
Families, for the first time, could drive
to another town on a Sunday.

55
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:02,444
But Ford noticed something that troubled
him.

56
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:06,715
The demand was enormous, and the factory
was too slow.

57
00:04:07,514 --> 00:04:13,616
Every car was built by hand, one at a
time, by skilled workers walking around a
stationary frame.

58
00:04:14,415 --> 00:04:19,710
If he wanted a car for everyone, he would
need to build them like nothing had ever
been built before.

59
00:04:20,622 --> 00:04:27,011
In nineteen thirteen, at the Highland Park
plant, Ford's engineers watched a
meatpacking idea in action:

60
00:04:27,276 --> 00:04:31,555
a moving line, with each worker doing a
single task as the work came to him.

61
00:04:32,356 --> 00:04:35,620
They tried it on a small part first, a
magneto.

62
00:04:36,419 --> 00:04:37,742
Then on engines.

63
00:04:38,541 --> 00:04:41,558
Then, in the fall, on the entire chassis.

64
00:04:42,356 --> 00:04:44,240
The result was almost frightening.

65
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:50,308
A car that once took more than twelve
hours to build now rolled out in about
ninety-three minutes.

66
00:04:51,106 --> 00:04:52,716
Prices began to fall.

67
00:04:53,515 --> 00:04:58,094
Eight hundred and fifty dollars became
five hundred and ninety, then four hundred
and ninety,

68
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,427
then lower, again and again, year after
year.

69
00:05:02,226 --> 00:05:07,873
Every time Ford cut the price, he sold
more cars, and every time he sold more
cars,

70
00:05:07,994 --> 00:05:09,357
his cost fell again.

71
00:05:10,155 --> 00:05:12,065
But the line had a hidden cost.

72
00:05:12,864 --> 00:05:15,542
The work was monotonous, and men hated it.

73
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:22,183
In nineteen thirteen, Ford had to hire
nearly ten new workers for every one that
he wanted to keep.

74
00:05:22,981 --> 00:05:24,956
The factory was bleeding talent.

75
00:05:25,754 --> 00:05:32,235
So on January fifth, nineteen fourteen, he
announced the five-dollar day, double the
going wage.

76
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:35,920
The press called it either socialism or
genius.

77
00:05:36,718 --> 00:05:37,651
It was neither.

78
00:05:38,450 --> 00:05:40,334
Here was the real calculation.

79
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:47,196
Ford had figured out that a worker who
earns enough to buy the product is the
best customer the company can ever have.

80
00:05:47,994 --> 00:05:49,370
Turnover collapsed.

81
00:05:50,169 --> 00:05:51,545
Productivity jumped.

82
00:05:52,343 --> 00:05:53,693
And profits went up.

83
00:05:54,606 --> 00:05:59,602
By the early nineteen twenties, Ford owned
the largest industrial empire on earth.

84
00:06:00,403 --> 00:06:03,536
The Model T was down to two hundred and
sixty dollars.

85
00:06:04,335 --> 00:06:06,961
Half of all the cars in the world were
Fords.

86
00:06:07,759 --> 00:06:09,591
And with power came control.

87
00:06:10,389 --> 00:06:16,844
In nineteen nineteen, he bought out every
shareholder, including the Dodge brothers,
who had helped fund him.

88
00:06:17,642 --> 00:06:21,766
The price was about one hundred and six
million dollars, and from that day,

89
00:06:21,965 --> 00:06:24,331
the company belonged to Henry Ford alone.

90
00:06:25,129 --> 00:06:30,164
He built the giant River Rouge complex,
where iron ore and coal came in one end,

91
00:06:30,350 --> 00:06:32,417
and finished cars came out the other.

92
00:06:33,215 --> 00:06:37,508
But the same man who understood wages so
well could not understand people.

93
00:06:38,306 --> 00:06:43,159
He insisted on controlling not only his
workers' hours, but their private lives.

94
00:06:43,957 --> 00:06:50,073
He published a newspaper, the Dearborn
Independent, that spread hateful lies
against Jewish Americans,

95
00:06:50,259 --> 00:06:52,950
and the damage it caused was real and
lasting.

96
00:06:53,749 --> 00:06:58,341
Years later, Ford apologized, but the
stain never fully washed away.

97
00:06:59,139 --> 00:07:05,008
He refused to change the Model T for
almost two decades while competitors, led
by General Motors,

98
00:07:05,142 --> 00:07:07,651
offered color, comfort, and choice.

99
00:07:08,449 --> 00:07:15,437
By nineteen twenty-seven, sales had
collapsed, and he had to shut down the
entire plant for months to launch the
Model A.

100
00:07:16,236 --> 00:07:21,961
His own son, Edsel, tried to modernize the
company, and Henry fought him at every
turn.

101
00:07:22,759 --> 00:07:26,414
Edsel died in nineteen forty-three, at
only forty-nine.

102
00:07:27,212 --> 00:07:30,763
Many people close to the family believed
the stress had broken him.

103
00:07:31,686 --> 00:07:33,492
Then the world changed again.

104
00:07:34,292 --> 00:07:38,989
When the Second World War began, Ford, who
had once been a loud pacifist,

105
00:07:39,162 --> 00:07:41,931
turned his factories into the arsenal of
democracy.

106
00:07:42,730 --> 00:07:48,025
At Willow Run, near Detroit, he built a
plant almost a mile long, with one goal:

107
00:07:48,238 --> 00:07:52,127
to produce the B-twenty-four bomber the
way he had once produced the Model T.

108
00:07:52,170 --> 00:07:53,195
Critics laughed.

109
00:07:53,993 --> 00:07:56,215
A bomber has over a million parts.

110
00:07:57,014 --> 00:07:58,911
It cannot be built like a car.

111
00:07:59,709 --> 00:08:01,567
Early on, they were right.

112
00:08:02,365 --> 00:08:07,192
The plant struggled, and workers called it
Willow Run, the place where they never
run.

113
00:08:07,990 --> 00:08:09,965
But the machine slowly caught up.

114
00:08:10,764 --> 00:08:14,575
At its peak, a bomber rolled off that line
every single hour.

115
00:08:15,373 --> 00:08:18,806
Inside the company, though, the old man
was fading.

116
00:08:19,605 --> 00:08:30,213
In nineteen thirty-seven, his security
chief, Harry Bennett, had beaten union
organizers outside the River Rouge plant
in a brutal clash that the press called
the Battle of the Overpass.

117
00:08:31,011 --> 00:08:32,960
Photographs shocked the country.

118
00:08:33,759 --> 00:08:39,028
Ford held out against the union for four
more years, until a strike forced him to
give in.

119
00:08:39,826 --> 00:08:44,523
After Edsel died, the eighty-year-old
founder returned to run the company
himself.

120
00:08:45,321 --> 00:08:48,025
It was losing about ten million dollars a
month.

121
00:08:48,824 --> 00:08:53,767
Only a family intervention, led by his
wife Clara and his grandson Henry the
Second,

122
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:57,075
forced him to hand over control in
nineteen forty-five.

123
00:08:57,873 --> 00:09:00,069
The young grandson saved the company.

124
00:09:00,868 --> 00:09:02,361
The old man went home.

125
00:09:03,294 --> 00:09:10,048
Henry Ford died on April seventh, nineteen
forty-seven, at the age of eighty-three,
during a power failure,

126
00:09:10,208 --> 00:09:11,962
in a house lit by candles.

127
00:09:12,762 --> 00:09:15,662
The empire he built had put the world on
wheels.

128
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:18,396
His story gives two lessons.

129
00:09:19,195 --> 00:09:22,862
The first is that a great company does not
just sell to customers.

130
00:09:23,661 --> 00:09:25,245
It builds the customers.

131
00:09:26,044 --> 00:09:30,310
When Ford paid workers enough to buy his
cars, he made his own market.

132
00:09:31,109 --> 00:09:32,563
The second is a warning.

133
00:09:33,361 --> 00:09:38,279
The same stubbornness that made him a
giant, refusing to give up, refusing to
listen,

134
00:09:38,518 --> 00:09:42,498
refusing to share control, almost
destroyed everything he created.

135
00:09:43,296 --> 00:09:49,008
Every great builder needs one thing he
rarely wants: someone brave enough to tell
him he is wrong.

136
00:09:49,807 --> 00:09:55,623
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe,
because tomorrow at six, another file
opens.

